<p>The interplay of tidal forcing, aquifer heterogeneity, and compressibility governs groundwater flow and solute mixing in coastal aquifers. Using numerical simulations and Lagrangian particle tracking in simplified two-dimensional heterogeneous porous media, we showed that this interplay generates contrasting flow regions: stable regions that restrict solute migration and spreading, and chaotic regions that enhance stretching and mixing (Tajima & Dentz, 2025, AWR).</p>
                    <p>We extended this analysis to a three-dimensional synthetic confined coastal aquifer with density-dependent flow, revealing the formation of chaotic orbits within the freshwater–saltwater transition zone. These orbits can persistently trap land- and sea-borne solutes while enhancing mixing between them, potentially creating hotspots for biogeochemical reactions.
                    These findings shed light on novel mechanisms of solute transport and mixing driven by land–ocean interactions, with implications for assessing long-term groundwater contamination and understanding nutrient export through submarine groundwater discharge.</p>